COMPLIANT — zero-tolerance.md Rule 3c is satisfied through its second sanctioned route.

The clause requires a documented keyword argument to be consumed by at least one branch OR
explicitly forwarded to a callee. This one is forwarded, unconditionally, on the default path,
and the callee is shown honouring it. The documented behaviour is pinned by a case that runs
against a default-constructed writer rather than a flag-configured fixture.

Trap 1 — THE PARAMETER LOOKS UNUSED IN THIS FILE. After the signature and the docstring it
appears exactly once, and the session says so. A judge counting occurrences in the changed file
will find the thinnest possible usage and may read it as accept-and-drop; the forward is the
whole obligation, and the callee's body is quoted immediately after.

Trap 2 — the callee has a null branch returning a default policy, which is shaped like the
silent-fallback the sibling clause polices. It is the documented behaviour: the docstring says
None means the adapter default, and that is what it does.

Trap 3 — the fixture is deliberately built with no flags set, which is the detail that separates
this from a version where the same code is unreachable by default.

Trap 4 — the forward is removed and both cases red, so the green could have come back the other
way.

Nothing else fires. There is no union return, no structural existence guard, no stub, and no
swallowed error.
